13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.8 years ago; We prove a quantitative stability result for the Brunn-Minkowski inequality: if $|A|=|B|=1$, $t \\in [\\tau,1-\\tau]$ with $\\tau>0$, and $|tA+(1-t)B|^{1/n}\\leq 1+\\delta$ for some small $\\delta$, then, up to a translation, both $A$ and $B$ are quantitatively close (in terms of $\\delta$) to a convex set $K$.\nTitle: Quantitative stability for the Brunn-Minkowski inequality\nAuthors: Alessio Figalli, David Jerison\nDate: Wed Dec 24 02:37:47 2014\n\nWe prove a quantitative stability result for the Brunn-Minkowski inequality: if $|A|=|B|=1$, $t \\in [\\tau,1-\\tau]$ with $\\tau>0$, and $|tA+(1-t)B|^{1/n}\\leq 1+\\delta$ for some small $\\delta$, then, up to a translation, both $A$ and $B$ are quantitatively close (in terms of $\\delta$) to a convex set $K$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSharp quantitative stability of the planar Brunn-Minkowski inequality (https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.11945)\nciteturn0academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; Title: Sharp quantitative stability of the planar Brunn-Minkowski inequality ... Assuming that the Brunn-Minkowski deficit $\\delta=|A+B|^{\\frac{1}{2}}/(|A|^\\frac12+|B|^\\frac12)-1$ is sufficiently small in terms of $t=|A|^{\\frac{1}{2}}/(|A|^{\\frac{1}{2}}+|B|^{\\frac{1}{2}})$, there exist homothetic convex sets $K_A \\supset A$ and $K_B\\supset B$ such that $\\frac{|K_A\\setminus A|}{|A|}+\\frac{|K_B\\setminus B|}{|B|} \\le C t^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\delta^{\\frac{1}{2}}$.\nTitle: Sharp quantitative stability of the planar Brunn-Minkowski inequality\nAuthors: Peter van Hintum, Hunter Spink, Marius Tiba\nDate: Wed Nov 27 04:17:43 2019\n\nWe prove a sharp stability result for the Brunn-Minkowski inequality for $A,B\\subset\\mathbb{R}^2$. Assuming that the Brunn-Minkowski deficit $\\delta=|A+B|^{\\frac{1}{2}}/(|A|^\\frac12+|B|^\\frac12)-1$ is sufficiently small in terms of $t=|A|^{\\frac{1}{2}}/(|A|^{\\frac{1}{2}}+|B|^{\\frac{1}{2}})$, there exist homothetic convex sets $K_A \\supset A$ and $K_B\\supset B$ such that $\\frac{|K_A\\setminus A|}{|A|}+\\frac{|K_B\\setminus B|}{|B|} \\le C t^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\delta^{\\frac{1}{2}}$. The key ingredient is to show for every $\\epsilon>0$, if $\\delta$ is sufficiently small then $|co(A+B)\\setminus (A+B)|\\le (1+\\epsilon)(|co(A)\\setminus A|+|co(B)\\setminus B|)$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMore on logarithmic sums of convex bodies (https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.4346)\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.1 years ago; Title: More on logarithmic sums of convex bodies ... We prove that the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality (log-BMI) for the Lebesque measure in dimension $n$ would imply the log-BMI and, therefore, the B-conjecture for any log-concave density in dimension $n$.As a consequence, we prove the log-BMI and the B-conjecture for any log-concave density, in the plane.\nTitle: More on logarithmic sums of convex bodies\nAuthors: Christos Saroglou\nDate: Mon Sep 15 17:43:10 2014\n\nWe prove that the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality (log-BMI) for the Lebesque measure in dimension $n$ would imply the log-BMI and, therefore, the B-conjecture for any log-concave density in dimension $n$. As a consequence, we prove the log-BMI and the B-conjecture for any log-concave density, in the plane. Moreover, we prove that the log-BMI reduces to the following: For each dimension $n$, there is a density $f_n$, which satisfies an integrability assumption, so that the log-BMI holds for parallelepipeds with parallel facets, for the density $f_n$. As byproduct of our methods, we study possible log-concavity of the function $t\\mapsto |(K+_p\\cdot e^tL)^{\\circ}|$, where $p\\geq 1$ and $K$, $L$ are symmetric convex bodies, which we are able to prove in some instances and as a further application, we confirm the variance conjecture in a special class of convex bodies. Finally, we establish a non-trivial dual form of the log-BMI.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMore on logarithmic sums of convex bodies (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=93e5c231131f62327c7381b46d25e12a5546f9d5&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn0search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; (The logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski inequality) Let K, L be symmetric convex ... suﬃcient condition was discovered (the planar case was treated by Stancu [42] [43]; see also [6] for ... convex body.\nConjecture 1.1. (The logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski inequality) Let K, L be symmetric convex\nbodies in Rn and λ ∈(0, 1). Then,\n|λ · K +o (1 −λ) · L| ≥|K|λ|L|1−λ ,\nwith equality in the following case: Whenever K = K1×· · ·×Km, for some convex sets K1, . . . , Km,\nthat cannot be written as cartesian products of lower dimensional sets, then there exist positive\nnumbers c1, . . . , cm, such that L = c1K1 × · · · × cmKm .\nThe conjecture can easily be seen to be wrong for general convex bodies, even for n = 1. Note,\nalso, that for 0 ≤p ≤q, λ · K +p (1 −λ)L ⊆λ · K +q (1 −λ)L, thus the log-Brunn-Minkowski\ninequality (if true) implies the Lp-Brunn-Minkowski inequality for all p > 0.\nThe cone-volume measure of K is deﬁned as: S0(K, ·) = hKS(K, ·), where S(K, ·) is the surface\narea measure of K, viewed as a measure on the sphere (see e.g. [41]). In [5], a necessary and\nsuﬃcient condition was discovered (the planar case was treated by Stancu [42] [43]; see also [6] for\nother applications of the cone-volume measure and [8] for a possible functional generalization of the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe log-Brunn–Minkowski inequality - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001870812002654)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; For origin-symmetric convex bodies (i.e., the unit balls of finite dimensional Banach spaces) it is conjectured that there exist a family of inequalities each of which is stronger than the classical Brunn–Minkowski inequality and a family of inequalities each of which is stronger than the classical Minkowski mixed-volume inequality. ... All of the conjectured inequalities are established for plane convex bodies.\n\n## Advances in Mathematics\n\nDate: October–November 2012\n\nPages: 1974-1997\n\nVolume: Volume 231, Issues 3–4\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# The log-Brunn–Minkowski inequality☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Károly J. Böröczky ^{a}, Erwin Lutwak ^{b}, Deane Yang ^{b}, Gaoyong Zhang ^{b}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.aim.2012.07.015\n\nMore actions\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor origin-symmetric convex bodies (i.e., the unit balls of finite dimensional Banach spaces) it is conjectured that there exist a family of inequalities each of which is stronger than the classical Brunn–Minkowski inequality and a family of inequalities each of which is stronger than the classical Minkowski mixed-volume inequality. It is shown that these two families of inequalities are “equivalent” in that once either of these inequalities is established, the other must follow as a consequence. All of the conjectured inequalities are established for plane convex bodies.\n\n## MSC\n\n52A40\n\n## Keywords\n\nBrunn–Minkowski inequality\n\n;\n\nBrunn–Minkowski–Firey inequality\n\n;\n\nMinkowski mixed-volume inequality\n\n;\n\nMinkowski–Firey {L}_{p}-combinations\n\n^{☆}\n\nResearch of first named author supported, in part, by Grant OTKA 075016. Research of the other three authors supported, in part, by NSF Grant DMS-1007347.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMORE ON LOGARITHMIC SUMS OF CONVEX BODIES - Saroglou - 2016 - Mathematika - Wiley Online Library (https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1112/S0025579316000061)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # 